We define the squares $K_1, K_2, K_3, \ldots$ as follows:
• $K_1$ is a square with side length $a$;
• $K_2$ is a square each vertex of which lies on a different side of the square $K_1$ and divides this side in the ratio $1:3$;
• $K_3$ is a square each vertex of which lies on a different side of the square $K_2$ and divides this side in the ratio $1:3$;
and in general, for every natural number $n\ge 2$,
• $K_n$ is a square each vertex of which lies on a different side of the square $K_{n-1}$ and divides this side in the ratio $1:3$.
The perimeters of all the squares defined above form an infinite geometric sequence. The figure shows squares constructed in the way described above.
Compute the sum of all terms of this infinite sequence.

